1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:20,000
 I was extraordinarily lucky, and happened to be at the Xerox research, Xerox Palo Alto

2
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:26,100
 Research Center, when a problem evolved that had never before occurred. The problem had

3
00:00:26,100 --> 00:00:32,200
 never occurred, and that was the problem of having a building full of personal computers.

4
00:00:32,200 --> 00:00:37,640
 I was the networking guy, so they turned to me and said, "Network these puppies." Now,

5
00:00:37,640 --> 00:00:43,480
 we had just finished starting the internet, it was then called the ARPANET, which was

6
00:00:43,480 --> 00:00:49,680
 packet switching, and it was pretty clear we wanted this network to connect to the wasn't

7
00:00:49,680 --> 00:00:55,080
 yet called internet thing, so it would be packet switched, we were pretty sure. At the

8
00:00:55,080 --> 00:01:00,160
 same time, we were building arguably, I don't want to get into that argument, the first

9
00:01:00,160 --> 00:01:06,240
 laser printer, which in our case, the first one, whose name was EARS, that's a whole

10
00:01:06,240 --> 00:01:11,720
 other story. It was a page per second, 500 dots per inch, and if you do the math, that's

11
00:01:11,720 --> 00:01:19,560
 about 20 megabits per second. So the existing methods of interconnection had a lot of problems.

12
00:01:19,560 --> 00:01:23,680
 One is they were all home run, so all these wires, one from every desk, would all come

13
00:01:23,680 --> 00:01:28,300
 to this one place in the building and put a big rat's nest, we called it a rat's nest

14
00:01:28,300 --> 00:01:35,520
 by the way. Two is they generally ran at 300 bits per second, or if you really rev them

15
00:01:35,520 --> 00:01:41,360
 up, they go to 14 for kilobits per second, and that wasn't even close to 20 megabits

16
00:01:41,360 --> 00:01:45,400
 per second, so we wanted to be able to keep the printer busy by sending documents from

17
00:01:45,400 --> 00:01:51,680
 all these PCs, which hadn't been built yet, except, well, they hadn't been built even

18
00:01:51,680 --> 00:01:56,480
 at PARC at that point. So we were building the printer and we were building the PCs all

19
00:01:56,480 --> 00:02:05,720
 at the same time. So I started to work on this, and there was an effort before mine

20
00:02:05,720 --> 00:02:13,200
 called SIGNET at PARC, and it was being done by Charles Simony, my friend, but he wasn't

21
00:02:13,200 --> 00:02:18,560
 a networking guy, so they sent me in to pick up SIGNET from Charles, and he was going to

22
00:02:18,560 --> 00:02:22,880
 off and do something else, and by the way, the other thing he did is he wrote a text

23
00:02:22,880 --> 00:02:33,160
 editor, a word processor called BRAVO, which then became Microsoft Office. So Charles is

24
00:02:33,160 --> 00:02:39,000
 a billionaire, and he's been to the space station twice, the International Space Station.

25
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:43,760
 So maybe I shouldn't have kicked him off SIGNET, maybe I should have done BRAVO, I don't know.

26
00:02:43,760 --> 00:02:51,240
 Anyway, so I immediately decided that SIGNET, by the way, SIGNET stands for Simony's Infinitely

27
00:02:51,240 --> 00:03:00,540
 Glorious Network, was in fact infinitely glorious and had too many moving parts to be a LAN.

28
00:03:00,540 --> 00:03:07,200
 By the way, the word LAN wasn't invented until 1990, and this is still 1973, so the word

29
00:03:07,200 --> 00:03:12,840
 LAN was way in the future, so that's an anachronism. Anyway, the SIGNET had too many moving parts

30
00:03:12,840 --> 00:03:21,240
 for a LAN, and it was in the course of investigating how to organize this LAN that I ran into quite

31
00:03:21,240 --> 00:03:26,400
 by accident by a packet radio network at the University of Hawaii. It was called the Aloha

32
00:03:26,400 --> 00:03:33,200
 Net, and what was beautiful about the Aloha Network is it solved a distributed problem.

33
00:03:33,200 --> 00:03:40,200
 That is, how would we share a radio channel back to the mainframe in Hawaii at the university

34
00:03:40,200 --> 00:03:45,400
 if we were just a bunch of terminals scattered among the Hawaiian islands and they couldn't

35
00:03:45,400 --> 00:03:51,360
 really easily talk to each other to get coordinated? How would they coordinate their sharing of

36
00:03:51,360 --> 00:03:54,200
 this inbound radio channel?

37
00:03:54,200 --> 00:03:58,040
 And Norm Abramson there at the University of Hawaii devised this very simple randomized

38
00:03:58,040 --> 00:04:03,680
 retransmission procedure where a person would type, by the way, what they would type was

39
00:04:03,680 --> 00:04:11,520
 a card image. It was 80 columns wide back from the days of card batch processing. So

40
00:04:11,520 --> 00:04:18,000
 you would type in your card image and hit send, and then your terminal would send it

41
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:23,360
 in toward the mainframe and then would wait a short time to see if there was an acknowledgement

42
00:04:23,360 --> 00:04:28,320
 returned on the outbound channel. And if there was, everything was hunky dory, but if there

43
00:04:28,320 --> 00:04:35,040
 wasn't, that probably meant the two terminals had decided to send at the same time. So then

44
00:04:35,040 --> 00:04:42,080
 as many terminals as participated in that collision of transmissions would then randomize

45
00:04:42,080 --> 00:04:46,880
 and then retransmit at a random time in the future. Thereby, if they overlapped here,

46
00:04:46,880 --> 00:04:50,920
 they very likely would not overlap again in the future because they would choose different

47
00:04:50,920 --> 00:04:57,840
 random numbers to count down. So that's randomized retransmission multiple access.

48
00:04:57,840 --> 00:05:02,400
 Well, since I was trying to avoid this big rat's nest of wires and only wanted to have

49
00:05:02,400 --> 00:05:11,000
 one wire, just one wire, not 16 or 32 or whatever the alternatives were, just one, I wanted

50
00:05:11,000 --> 00:05:17,560
 a distributed solution to how to share this cable and the ALOHA network produce that using

51
00:05:17,560 --> 00:05:24,520
 randomized retransmissions. So then I actually, you know, there's sort of two stories here.

52
00:05:24,520 --> 00:05:29,440
 One is a hardware story, there's a hardware story and there's a software story. And the

53
00:05:29,440 --> 00:05:35,120
 hardware story is not that interesting, but it's there. So one of the first things I did

54
00:05:35,120 --> 00:05:40,760
 was to buy a kilometer of cable, or maybe a mile, I don't know if I'd gone metric yet.

55
00:05:40,760 --> 00:05:49,140
 So a spool of coax cable about this big with the two ends sticking up. So I got a pulse

56
00:05:49,140 --> 00:05:53,600
 generator and I hooked it up to one end and hooked it around back into the oscilloscope

57
00:05:53,600 --> 00:05:58,760
 and started launching square waves down the cable and watching what came out. I thought

58
00:05:58,760 --> 00:06:03,000
 that would be good preparation for building a network. And what came out the other side

59
00:06:03,000 --> 00:06:10,400
 wasn't a square wave. It was sort of lazy rise times and lazy fall times. But if you

60
00:06:10,400 --> 00:06:17,120
 put a digital gate, you could recover the square wave. That is, you just set, use the

61
00:06:17,120 --> 00:06:21,680
 digital threshold and so out of this gate came a square wave. So you could recover at

62
00:06:21,680 --> 00:06:27,840
 the end of a mile of cable this square wave. So I kind of knew, I kind of had some confidence

63
00:06:27,840 --> 00:06:33,920
 then that we, that various stations connected to this cable could inject their square waves

64
00:06:33,920 --> 00:06:44,740
 and the other guys could recover them. So that hardware wasn't that hard. It was a straightforward.

65
00:06:44,740 --> 00:06:47,640
 So the first hardware was kind of relatively straightforward.

66
00:06:47,640 --> 00:06:52,400
 And the square wave, by the way, was called Manchester. What we'd do is we'd take the

67
00:06:52,400 --> 00:06:58,320
 bit, we'd make a packet of bits. And then we'd send them one at a time down this cable

68
00:06:58,320 --> 00:07:02,400
 and we would encode them. And the encoding was also simple. It was Manchester coding

69
00:07:02,400 --> 00:07:08,880
 meaning for each bit, the first half of the bit would be the complement of the bit and

70
00:07:08,880 --> 00:07:12,920
 the second half of the bit would be the bit. So you would have a transition in the middle

71
00:07:12,920 --> 00:07:19,340
 of each bit cell, which is a very simple modulation scheme. So as you're sending the packet, the

72
00:07:19,340 --> 00:07:23,240
 cable's wiggling and you can recover the signal at the other end and clock those bits into

73
00:07:23,240 --> 00:07:27,920
 a shift register and then clock the shift register into the memory and collect the packet

74
00:07:27,920 --> 00:07:28,920
 that way.

75
00:07:28,920 --> 00:07:33,000
 And that all took just some soldering to make that all happen basically.

76
00:07:33,000 --> 00:07:37,760
 Well I don't want to. It got a little bit complicated when you wanted to put 255 of

77
00:07:37,760 --> 00:07:42,720
 them on a mile of cable. You had to be a little bit careful about the impedance of the taps

78
00:07:42,720 --> 00:07:48,120
 because a square wave coming by a tap might generate reflections that would then interfere.

79
00:07:48,120 --> 00:07:52,000
 But the beauty of Manchester encoding was that while you were sending a packet, there

80
00:07:52,000 --> 00:07:57,880
 were constant transitions. So if you listened, you could tell whether a packet was going

81
00:07:57,880 --> 00:08:03,320
 by and you didn't have to listen for long. You only had to listen for about a bit time

82
00:08:03,320 --> 00:08:07,920
 which turned out to be 340 nanoseconds. So you could wait that long and tell whether

83
00:08:07,920 --> 00:08:13,280
 there was a packet going by. So one of the first differences between the Ethernet and

84
00:08:13,280 --> 00:08:19,940
 the ALOHA network, there are a lot of differences, but one of the first ones was carrier sense.

85
00:08:19,940 --> 00:08:23,880
 In the ALOHA network, you couldn't tell if somebody else was transmitting at the same

86
00:08:23,880 --> 00:08:27,840
 time as you, but on the Ethernet you could. And the advantage of that was you might as

87
00:08:27,840 --> 00:08:31,120
 well, if you're sending and somebody else is sending at the same time, you might as

88
00:08:31,120 --> 00:08:35,720
 well give up because you've destroyed each other's packets or punt. Then that recovers

89
00:08:35,720 --> 00:08:42,280
 that bandwidth that would otherwise be lost to just continuing to transmit a damaged packet.

90
00:08:42,280 --> 00:08:48,280
 And the other thing was this Manchester code meant that the cable was on half the time

91
00:08:48,280 --> 00:08:53,520
 and off half the time. That is, the driver, sort of an open, what we used to call an open

92
00:08:53,520 --> 00:08:59,420
 collector driver, would either yank the cable up to 3 or 4 or 5 volts, I even forget the

93
00:08:59,420 --> 00:09:06,200
 voltage now. But it was under 5, I'm sure of that. I had a rule, I never went above 5 volts.

94
00:09:06,200 --> 00:09:10,440
 You would yank the cable up and then during the other half the bit, when you weren't yanking,

95
00:09:10,440 --> 00:09:17,260
 you let go. So for example, in each bit cell, you got to look to see if anyone else was

96
00:09:17,260 --> 00:09:20,920
 transmitting when you had stopped. And if there was somebody else, then you had a collision.

97
00:09:20,920 --> 00:09:22,400
 So that was the second feature.

98
00:09:22,400 --> 00:09:26,300
 So it was really a digital signal. It was really a digital signal. It wasn't a modulated

99
00:09:26,300 --> 00:09:27,920
 signal in any way, right?

100
00:09:27,920 --> 00:09:29,560
 No. Well, it was...

101
00:09:29,560 --> 00:09:32,040
 Best you could send a digital signal over coaxing.

102
00:09:32,040 --> 00:09:37,800
 On off with the Manchester encoding. So the Manchester encoding is akin to a modulation

103
00:09:37,800 --> 00:09:39,800
 scheme, but it's the simplest one you can think of.

104
00:09:39,800 --> 00:09:40,800
 It's very baseband.

105
00:09:40,800 --> 00:09:45,720
 Yeah, it is very baseband, but it's a sort of modulation scheme that a computer scientist

106
00:09:45,720 --> 00:09:48,720
 would come up with as opposed to one of those fancy...

107
00:09:48,720 --> 00:09:49,720
 Radio people.

108
00:09:49,720 --> 00:09:50,720
 Radio people.

109
00:09:50,720 --> 00:09:51,720
 Right.

110
00:09:51,720 --> 00:09:54,480
 And by the way, I took a lot of gas from all those radio people. Why did you use such a

111
00:09:54,480 --> 00:09:59,240
 simple scheme? You wasted... You would have wasted all that band. You wasted all that

112
00:09:59,240 --> 00:10:03,920
 band with that capable... That cable was capable of carrying hundreds of megabits per second

113
00:10:03,920 --> 00:10:07,600
 and you only carried 2.94. What a waste of the cable.

114
00:10:07,600 --> 00:10:08,600
 Exactly.

115
00:10:08,600 --> 00:10:15,120
 Well, there was plenty of cable to waste. So we've talked about carrier sense and collision

116
00:10:15,120 --> 00:10:21,760
 detection and the scheme would be that each packet would carry two addresses, the address

117
00:10:21,760 --> 00:10:27,560
 of the destination and the address of the source. And each of these would be eight bits.

118
00:10:27,560 --> 00:10:33,800
 And so on the back plane of the little personal computers with wire app, you would wire up

119
00:10:33,800 --> 00:10:38,840
 in a code between zero and 255 and that would be the serial number of the machine. And then

120
00:10:38,840 --> 00:10:43,740
 you'd read that off the back plane and put it in the packet. So each packet had two addresses.

121
00:10:43,740 --> 00:10:48,120
 This is different from the alone incidentally, which had one address because the channel

122
00:10:48,120 --> 00:10:56,280
 was only going through two channels. So two addresses. And we added cyclic redundancy

123
00:10:56,280 --> 00:11:03,480
 checksum on the end of the packet, which we implemented in hardware so that you, in addition...

124
00:11:03,480 --> 00:11:09,400
 So you could tell if the packet had been damaged. So if there was a collision and the terminal,

125
00:11:09,400 --> 00:11:13,920
 the contending stations backed off, there would be a hunk of garbage on the cable zipping

126
00:11:13,920 --> 00:11:17,800
 around. But when it was received, the checksum wouldn't add up and you'd just throw that chunk

127
00:11:17,800 --> 00:11:21,480
 of garbage away. The day that I was launching pulses down this

128
00:11:21,480 --> 00:11:29,800
 spool of cable, I was doing some soldering and I was doing some knife work to get the

129
00:11:29,800 --> 00:11:36,800
 insulation off the copper. And across the room was a young grad student who was doing

130
00:11:36,800 --> 00:11:42,960
 something else and he noticed me not being good at this. And it turns out he was very

131
00:11:42,960 --> 00:11:47,160
 good at it because he had worked in a television studio and he had worked in cable television.

132
00:11:47,160 --> 00:11:52,000
 So he knew all about skinning wires and coaxes. So he came over to help me. And his name was

133
00:11:52,000 --> 00:11:58,280
 David Boggs. And then we started working together and invented

134
00:11:58,280 --> 00:12:06,760
 Ethernet together. And he was slightly more hardware and I was slightly more software.

135
00:12:06,760 --> 00:12:12,120
 But there was then a third guy who was even more hardware than David, who was the PicoFarad

136
00:12:12,120 --> 00:12:18,480
 guy, the guy who had put those last few passive components on the end just to be sure that

137
00:12:18,480 --> 00:12:26,640
 connection to the coaxial cable would be clean for transmissions. Every time you tapped into

138
00:12:26,640 --> 00:12:28,720
 it, you didn't put a big lump of impedance on it.

139
00:12:28,720 --> 00:12:33,960
 The tapping seems like a counterintuitive thing to me. I mean, everyone would think

140
00:12:33,960 --> 00:12:38,400
 that a star is the right thing, but you were going to tap into that, just tap into it.

141
00:12:38,400 --> 00:12:42,320
 One of the problems we decided to solve was the rat's nest problem. We did not want a

142
00:12:42,320 --> 00:12:46,340
 rat's nest. And every time we installed a new PC, we didn't have to home run a cable

143
00:12:46,340 --> 00:12:50,920
 back to the rat's nest. So we wanted to put one cable down the middle of the corridor.

144
00:12:50,920 --> 00:12:54,800
 And then every time you want to put a PC, you just run up and tap into it. And we didn't

145
00:12:54,800 --> 00:12:59,760
 want the network to go down while you were tapping into it because we wanted 24x7 access

146
00:12:59,760 --> 00:13:05,040
 to the network. So there had to be a way to tap into the network without bringing it down.

147
00:13:05,040 --> 00:13:12,240
 So that led to a device we found in the cable TV industry. It was called a Gerald tap. It

148
00:13:12,240 --> 00:13:20,080
 was basically a vampire tap. You'd drill a little hole in the outer casing of the coax

149
00:13:20,080 --> 00:13:24,640
 and then you would screw in this tap that would puncture the insulation and go right

150
00:13:24,640 --> 00:13:29,600
 to the copper and tap in. And you notice in that operation, you're not breaking the copper.

151
00:13:29,600 --> 00:13:33,600
 So the network continues sending. You tap in and you're now part of the network.

152
00:13:33,600 --> 00:13:38,300
 So that came from the cable industry. It did. So a guy named David Liddell, who had done

153
00:13:38,300 --> 00:13:45,680
 cable TV installations when he was in grad school in Toledo, suggested that we use the

154
00:13:45,680 --> 00:13:49,360
 Gerald tap since it was already being made in volume and worked just fine, as far as

155
00:13:49,360 --> 00:13:54,440
 he was concerned. And it allowed us to solve the rat's nest, what we perceive to be an

156
00:13:54,440 --> 00:14:00,120
 important problem, the rat's nest problem. The ALOHA network ran in the kilobit per second

157
00:14:00,120 --> 00:14:09,720
 range like 4800 or kilobits per second. Ethernet then started at 2.94 megabit. And in those

158
00:14:09,720 --> 00:14:19,520
 days, by the way, T1 was 1.544 megabits per second. So in 1973, Ethernet was already twice

159
00:14:19,520 --> 00:14:29,800
 as fast as T1. Of course, T1 is still around, oddly. But then we went from 2.94, we briefly

160
00:14:29,800 --> 00:14:37,040
 went to 20 megabits per second inside of Xerox. And then when we bumped into DEC and Intel

161
00:14:37,040 --> 00:14:42,760
 for the standardization process in 802, we decided on 10. We came down from 20 to 10

162
00:14:42,760 --> 00:14:48,680
 so the chips would work. And then we went from 10 and later I helped found a company

163
00:14:48,680 --> 00:14:54,040
 called Grand Junction Networks that introduced the 100 meg Ethernet. And I remember being

164
00:14:54,040 --> 00:15:01,000
 at my coffee table in Palo Alto, I think it was, I forget the year, no, maybe it was in

165
00:15:01,000 --> 00:15:08,720
 Woodside. But we were trying to think of how we would make a faster Ethernet. And there

166
00:15:08,720 --> 00:15:15,000
 is some math that shows that as you go faster, the efficiency depends on the diameter of

167
00:15:15,000 --> 00:15:20,440
 the network. And as you go faster and faster, the efficiency goes down, the diameter of

168
00:15:20,440 --> 00:15:26,240
 the network in bits. And we were trying to go up a factor of 10 and then, I don't know

169
00:15:26,240 --> 00:15:31,920
 who it was, one of us observed that, "Wait a minute, we've been assuming that Ethernet

170
00:15:31,920 --> 00:15:38,420
 is a kilometer in diameter, but we're all going to hubs now so that we only need a hundred

171
00:15:38,420 --> 00:15:42,640
 meters, not a thousand meters." And that was the factor of 10 right there. So by changing

172
00:15:42,640 --> 00:15:51,880
 the collision interval, you maintain the same theoretical efficiencies by just assuming

173
00:15:51,880 --> 00:15:55,520
 you're going 100 meters instead of a kilometer. So that got us to 100 megabits per second

174
00:15:55,520 --> 00:16:00,920
 and now gigabit, 10 gigabit, which I guess is the mainstream now, and then 100 gigabit

175
00:16:00,920 --> 00:16:08,480
 is now beginning to run, 100 gigabits per second. You can't be a computer scientist

176
00:16:08,480 --> 00:16:12,600
 and build that kind of digital now. You have to actually be a hardware engineer to run

177
00:16:12,600 --> 00:16:13,600
 that.

178
00:16:13,600 --> 00:16:19,080
 I think at that point you're pretty much a radio person.

179
00:16:19,080 --> 00:16:23,720
 But then after 100 gigabits comes terabit. So I've already begun giving talks on terabit

180
00:16:23,720 --> 00:16:24,240
 Ethernet.

